    {
      "content": "[SEG-aced80da89fa] stdio, communication over standard in and standard out\n\n[SEG-27b8e9d1e276] Clients SHOULD support stdio whenever possible. It is also possible for clients and servers to implement custom transports in a pluggable fashion.\n\n[SEG-d0d142c8b118] stdio: stdio\n\n[SEG-de8a13eca0e5] stdio: In the stdio transport:\n\n[SEG-6da2bf209f51] stdio: The client launches the MCP server as a subprocess.\n\n[SEG-ab1bd7ae2384] stdio: The server reads JSON-RPC messages from its standard input (stdin) and sends messages to its standard output (stdout).\n\n[SEG-fdd80999e743] stdio: Messages are individual JSON-RPC requests, notifications, or responses.\n\n[SEG-625bf507c14c] stdio: Messages are delimited by newlines, and MUST NOT contain embedded newlines.\n\n[SEG-a8f657b81b66] stdio: The server MAY write UTF-8 strings to its standard error (stderr) for any logging purposes including informational, debug, and error messages.\n\n[SEG-e0c991e6d2d9] stdio: The client MAY capture, forward, or ignore the server’s stderr output and SHOULD NOT assume stderr output indicates error conditions.\n\n[SEG-a2ee1a79dd00] stdio: The server MUST NOT write anything to its stdout that is not a valid MCP message.\n\n[SEG-8218f6b31f47] stdio: The client MUST NOT write anything to the server’s stdin that is not a valid MCP message.",
